e 20k, playing Black, won by 20 points after two passes. GPT-6.1 Sol was ahead in the middle game: after move 13 Black's lead stood at -9.1. Then B6, D7 and E8 lost 8.9, 8.6 and 8.2 points in turn, and Black's lead went from -9.1 to +10.4. The rest of the game was a slow slide that never came close to turning back.", "moments": [{"turn": 13, "text": "The 20k's G3 was its worst move, losing 7.3 points, and it took Black's lead from -1.8 to -9.1. The engine wanted G6. GPT-6.1 Sol was clearly ahead here, and the game was its to lose."}, {"turn": 14, "text": "GPT-6.1 Sol answered with B6, which lost 8.9 points and was its worst move of the game. The lead went from -9.1 back to -0.1. The engine's choice was G7. A nine-point edge was gone in one move."}, {"turn": 16, "text": "After the 20k's E6 (6.1 lost) put White back in front, D7 lost 8.6 points and turned a lead of -6.2 into +2.3 for Black. The engine preferred F7, which Black then took itself on move 17."}, {"turn": 18, "text": "E8 lost another 8.2 points, and Black's lead jumped from +2.2 to +10.4. The engine's choice was F8, and Black took that point next move. This was the third big loss in a row, and the game was effectively decided here."}, {"turn": 38, "text": "From move 28 to move 38, each GPT-6.1 Sol move lost between 1.6 and 2.1 points, and the engine named D6 every time. F4 lost 2.1 points. GPT-6.1 Sol only played D6 on move 40, when it no longer mattered, and the final margin was B+20."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "Everything it ran was plumbing: listing tools, reading files, and the stored `step` function, which only computes chains, liberties and captures for the single move the player named before calling play. The remaining commands replayed the finished game, built the SGF and wrote notes, and none of them looped over candidate moves or looked ahead.", "stop": false}}